course: dictionary
lesson: 5300
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the other words around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «From one wild cabbage plant, farmers bred cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, kale and Brussels sprouts.» Here kale is one of the foods, so it must be a vegetable.
explain: Kale also has an older meaning from Scotland. Long ago, kail or kale was an old word for cabbage soup, or for food in general. If a text is very old, or set in Scotland, look for clues. Is the writer talking about soup, a meal, or a plant in a garden?
explain: Use the swap check. Put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense. «The old Scots cook filled the pot with kale for supper.» Cabbage soup fits here, so that is the meaning the writer wants.
fact: Kale is a leafy green vegetable in the cabbage family, with curly or flat dark leaves.
fact: «Curly kale» and «a bunch of kale» are common phrases.
fact: Kale is uncountable, so we say some kale, not two kales.
fact: In Scotland, long ago, kail or kale meant cabbage soup or food in general.
fact: Broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts and kohlrabi are all one species, bred by farmers from wild cabbage.
exercise: «Farmers bred kale and Brussels sprouts from one wild cabbage plant.» What is kale in this sentence? | A type of cold winter wind | A small bird with a long tail | A tall tree in a park | *A leafy green vegetable in the cabbage family
exercise: «The old Scots cook filled the pot with kale for supper.» What does kale most likely mean here? | *Cabbage soup or food in general | A type of shoe | A school bell | A tall tree
exercise: Farmers bred several foods from wild cabbage. Which one is NOT in the list «broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts and kohlrabi»? | Kale | Broccoli | *Lettuce | Kohlrabi
test: Kale is uncountable. Which sentence is correct? | I bought a kales at the shop. | I bought two kales at the shop. | *I bought some kale at the shop.
test: «Curly kale» is a phrase. What does curly describe? | *The shape of the leaves | The name of the farm | The price of the vegetables | The colour of the soup
exam: Read this: «Broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, kale, Brussels sprouts and kohlrabi are all one species.» Why might a writer put kale in this list? | Because it is the only green food in the world | *Because it is one of several vegetables from the same wild cabbage | Because it is always eaten raw | Because it means a kind of bird
